      {
        "id": 187720,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-01-04T02:14:04.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"robb8888\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/r/dec6dc/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> robb8888:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What made you decide to skip a bunch of cards? &nbsp;I see people Mint-ing these B.S. rigged decks by using completely unconventional strategies – do you play the game on (ex:) Steam, see what cards are coming, then play it on another platform to get the Mint? &nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I go into every Weekly One-shot blind, not even glancing at the forum thread until I've played it once.</p>\n<p>My first turn I played Razor Ordnance and Flak Cannon, but I skipped for Fanatic because she had in hand a card that would deal no damage and heal us for 1 each, a card which would deal 1 damage and let her use an extra power she didn't have, and a card that would do nothing and get immediately destroyed, and a power that would deal no damage. &nbsp;Then I drew End of Days, and skipped Wraith's turn because anything she played would immediately get destroyed, and played Ammo Drop for Bunker to get all the card draw.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Knowing when to skip is in many ways the trickiest part of SotM. &nbsp;But Fanatic's hand was full of good cards that were situationally almost useless, and she had a useless power. &nbsp;In this case, skipping was definately the right choice. &nbsp;The fact that skipping in this case hands you Fanatic's favorite cure-all was gravy, but not necessary to make the decision.</p>",
        "post_number": 23,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:13:33.459Z",
